WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.610
Hi there.

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.910
It's Steve Kaufmann here and today I have
a guest that I'm really happy to meet up

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.390
with again, it's Rupa Sensei from Japan.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.440
But before we get into that,
uh, remember if you enjoy these

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.100
videos, please subscribe, click
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.880
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:21.990
Rupa.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.540
The last time we met was
in Osaka Japan two years.

00:00:29.045 --> 00:00:29.375
I think.

00:00:29.405 --> 00:00:30.605
Oh, time flies.

00:00:30.665 --> 00:00:31.445
Time flies.

00:00:31.745 --> 00:00:35.465
It was, it was, uh, we were at, my
son mark and I were on our way to

00:00:35.555 --> 00:00:39.215
Fukuoka for the, a polyglot conference.

00:00:39.215 --> 00:00:44.105
The first time that the international
polygon conference was held in, in

00:00:44.105 --> 00:00:45.754
Asia, in Japan, as it turns out.

00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:50.375
And, um, what struck me with
you and I think why I'm so happy

00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:51.305
to have you on my channel...

00:00:52.815 --> 00:00:57.075
first of all Japan, of course,
is, is a major country when

00:00:57.075 --> 00:00:58.515
it comes to English learning.

00:00:59.015 --> 00:01:02.625
I think Japan, I mean, there are
more people in Japan who take

00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:05.985
TOEIC than the rest of the world
combined or something like that.

00:01:06.015 --> 00:01:06.315
Wow.

00:01:06.375 --> 00:01:08.535
Tremendous country of TOEIC takers.

00:01:09.045 --> 00:01:13.425
And, and when I go to bookstores in
Japan, of course, there are books for

00:01:13.425 --> 00:01:16.695
learning other languages like Korean
and Chinese and Spanish and so forth,

00:01:16.725 --> 00:01:18.855
but overwhelmingly it's English.

00:01:19.185 --> 00:01:24.645
And I think you're very much involved with
helping Japanese people learn English.

00:01:24.645 --> 00:01:27.825
So could you begin first of all,
by giving, making a brief sort

00:01:27.825 --> 00:01:31.365
of introduction of, of what you
do and your activity there in

00:01:31.365 --> 00:01:33.015
Japan now, your presence online.

00:01:34.185 --> 00:01:34.695
Okay.

00:01:34.725 --> 00:01:35.895
Hey guys, how you doing?

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:42.600
I'm Rupa Sensei as I'm called on YouTube
and I teach English to Japanese people.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.554
As Mark said, I used to be a teacher
in Japan back about five years ago.

00:01:48.854 --> 00:01:52.005
And I just kind of realized
like just one classroom, you

00:01:52.005 --> 00:01:53.715
know, it's not enough for me.

00:01:53.774 --> 00:01:58.365
I want to make videos online
and teach the whole country or

00:01:58.725 --> 00:02:00.315
even the whole world one day.

00:02:00.315 --> 00:02:03.345
So that's why I started
making videos on YouTube.

00:02:03.375 --> 00:02:04.335
And I really like it.

00:02:04.335 --> 00:02:10.065
I think I found my passion in teaching
and not only teaching the language

00:02:10.484 --> 00:02:15.655
in its elements, but also about
motivation and also, yeah keeping up

00:02:15.655 --> 00:02:18.565
the enthusiasm to continue to learn.

00:02:19.255 --> 00:02:21.415
Well, you know, that's why I
wanted to have you on my channel.

00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:23.005
I had a discussion...

00:02:23.405 --> 00:02:23.855
Perfect

00:02:23.855 --> 00:02:28.649
...not too long ago with, with, uh,
I think his name was Alberto, an

00:02:28.649 --> 00:02:31.829
Italian guy who teaches online now.

00:02:31.829 --> 00:02:35.519
And he also was very enthusiastic
and I just think enthusiasm

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:37.709
is such an important quality.

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:42.489
And so often the language learners
seems to be so serious, he's seriously

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.679
studying, uh, you know, lists of
words or he's studying the grammar

00:02:46.679 --> 00:02:48.269
book or he's doing exercises.

00:02:49.325 --> 00:02:52.085
And that's not the solution really.

00:02:52.085 --> 00:02:56.614
I mean, the Japanese put a lot of
effort into those kinds of activities.

00:02:57.275 --> 00:03:02.525
And I think what you provide is sort of
this energy that allows them to break out

00:03:02.525 --> 00:03:04.685
of that mold and actually make progress.

00:03:05.475 --> 00:03:10.415
What has been the reaction to your style
sort of call it somewhat unorthodox

00:03:10.445 --> 00:03:15.315
style of English teaching in so far as
the Japanese learners are concerned?

00:03:16.945 --> 00:03:18.630
Well, so far, it's been pretty good.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:23.340
I've been doing YouTube and stuff
for about four years and it's been

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.380
going, it's been going really well.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:31.200
Um, yeah, I think definitely at the
start, uh, the kind of reason was

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:36.000
because I was teaching in Japan and I
did kind of see that what you said, the

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.100
very serious side of language learning
where people are so focused on tests

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.280
and studying long lists of vocabulary.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.730
And I just kind of asked some of
my students and really wondered to

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.010
myself, are these people having fun?

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:55.590
Are they actually doing the language
learning because they enjoy it or just

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:59.505
because the teacher's telling them,
just because if they don't do it, they

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.245
won't get into the good university?

00:04:01.245 --> 00:04:05.655
And I think that's a shame if it
is the latter, if they are just

00:04:05.655 --> 00:04:07.515
studying it without having fun.

00:04:07.515 --> 00:04:12.285
So I guess my goal or my mission
that I've been on is just create

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:14.085
things that are much more fun.

00:04:14.295 --> 00:04:19.885
And it all started back when I was a
teacher and I would just put on like,

00:04:20.154 --> 00:04:24.085
you know, show little scenes of Back
to the Future or something in class

00:04:24.085 --> 00:04:27.145
to fill a half an hour or 15 minutes.

00:04:27.355 --> 00:04:32.875
And during those periods, during those
classes, the students just, you know,

00:04:33.235 --> 00:04:36.965
their, theie, their faces would just
brighten up, their eyes would lighten up.

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:39.745
And I could tell that they were
just having a lot more fun than

00:04:39.985 --> 00:04:47.195
with there, you know, TOEIC, um,
preparation or the ... preparation,

00:04:47.534 --> 00:04:49.995
uh, entrance exam kind of preparation.

00:04:49.995 --> 00:04:54.375
And that's kind of why I started
to go down that road or that

00:04:54.375 --> 00:04:56.925
path, just making more fun things.

00:04:56.925 --> 00:05:00.495
And it's definitely been
received pretty well over there.

00:05:01.094 --> 00:05:02.354
You know, it's interesting.

00:05:02.445 --> 00:05:07.724
Uh, not all learning activities
are equally valuable and we could

00:05:08.325 --> 00:05:13.065
be doing things that we think
are learning activities and we

00:05:13.065 --> 00:05:14.145
may not be achieving very much.

00:05:14.985 --> 00:05:20.535
And the magic ingredient that makes
any learning activity that sort of, you

00:05:20.535 --> 00:05:27.225
know, multiplies the effectiveness is
that element of enjoyment, enthusiasm,

00:05:27.255 --> 00:05:29.715
liking it, interest all of these things.

00:05:30.075 --> 00:05:33.515
And it's very difficult for a
teacher to inject that I typically.

00:05:34.054 --> 00:05:38.104
You know, a classroom teacher with
30 kids in the classroom, half of

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:40.054
them are looking out the window.

00:05:40.054 --> 00:05:40.504
It's so...

00:05:40.504 --> 00:05:41.304
Sleepy.

00:05:41.564 --> 00:05:42.094
Hungry.

00:05:42.094 --> 00:05:42.374
Yeah.

00:05:42.395 --> 00:05:43.085
Yeah.

00:05:43.085 --> 00:05:47.585
So, but your people are the people
you who interact with you, who

00:05:47.585 --> 00:05:52.325
ask you questions, who come back
at you, they, they presumably

00:05:52.325 --> 00:05:54.125
are infected by your enthusiasm.

00:05:54.125 --> 00:05:56.315
And what sorts of questions
do they ask typically?

00:05:56.565 --> 00:05:57.705
What sorts of comments?

00:05:57.705 --> 00:06:00.315
What sort of feedback do
you get from your learners?

00:06:01.545 --> 00:06:06.465
Well, I think, um, the number one thing
or the number one goal that I have,

00:06:06.495 --> 00:06:09.705
especially with the YouTube videos and
everything that I make, I want to be

00:06:09.705 --> 00:06:13.185
kind of a gateway into a long-term path.

00:06:13.185 --> 00:06:16.425
So they might have a
little bit of interest.

00:06:16.724 --> 00:06:20.505
Uh, they, might've learned a little bit
of English at school but they've kind

00:06:20.505 --> 00:06:25.845
of forgotten it already and they kind
of want to start again from scratch, but

00:06:25.845 --> 00:06:30.645
they just don't know where to start or
they just haven't found fun material yet.

00:06:30.645 --> 00:06:34.155
And then they search it on YouTube
or on Google who are the, like the

00:06:34.155 --> 00:06:36.285
biggest, you know, searching platforms.

00:06:36.285 --> 00:06:37.785
And then they might come across my video.

00:06:38.414 --> 00:06:42.135
I just want to be a good first
impression to the language, right.

00:06:42.405 --> 00:06:46.724
Obviously I can only make, you know,
one or two videos a week, so I'm not

00:06:46.755 --> 00:06:50.534
all that they should be doing, but I
just want to be a good step in the right

00:06:50.534 --> 00:06:54.775
direction where it's like, okay, well
learning a language can actually be fun.

00:06:54.805 --> 00:06:57.044
Your videos then typically
what do they consist of?

00:06:57.974 --> 00:06:59.135
Maybe tell my viewers.

00:07:00.615 --> 00:07:05.190
Back in Japan um, I used to do a lot
of interviews, whether it's on the

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:10.710
street or, you know, kind of organized
interviews with someone and talk about

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:15.390
different cultures, culture shocks that
they have, uh, being in a new country.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:21.510
Um, I used to do random English tests
where we would have hidden cameras and we

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:26.770
would just go around talking to people in
English in Japan, ask them for directions.

00:07:26.985 --> 00:07:30.525
These days, you know, especially
due to the lockdown that we had and

00:07:30.525 --> 00:07:34.635
everything where I couldn't really
do those kinds of videos i, I focused

00:07:34.635 --> 00:07:39.415
more on learning with a movie snippets
where I might take a, a little movie...

00:07:40.815 --> 00:07:41.275
Yeah.

00:07:41.785 --> 00:07:45.255
Show about 15 seconds or 20
seconds, and then explain it

00:07:45.255 --> 00:07:46.605
together with the audience.

00:07:47.895 --> 00:07:48.675
Oh, that's really good.

00:07:48.675 --> 00:07:52.035
So for a while you were interviewing
people on the street, uh,

00:07:52.065 --> 00:07:54.165
foreigners or Japanese people.

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:58.170
Uh, but now you're working
more with new movie snippets.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:07:59.310
That's an awfully good idea.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.360
And then explaining some aspects
of the language or the culture

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:07.020
that's kind of embodied in those
movies snippets and what...

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:07.590
Exactly.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:08.100
Exactly.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:08.460
Yeah.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.340
And what's been the reaction
to those from your audience?

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:13.305
Going pretty good.

00:08:13.305 --> 00:08:14.325
It's going pretty good.

00:08:14.505 --> 00:08:15.525
Definitely.

00:08:15.615 --> 00:08:19.515
That's the good thing about YouTube is
you kind of know if something's working

00:08:19.725 --> 00:08:22.365
or not working, it kind of tells you.

00:08:23.415 --> 00:08:24.315
Absolutely.

00:08:24.435 --> 00:08:24.885
Yeah.

00:08:24.975 --> 00:08:25.575
Okay.

00:08:25.995 --> 00:08:30.630
And, uh, so yeah, the interesting thing
about YouTube is you have no idea what's

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.430
going to, which video is going to do well.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.970
You, you, you think you have
some idea, it's a mystery.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:37.590
It's it's, to me, it's still a mystery.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:38.670
Some videos do very well.

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:40.320
Some videos don't do very well at all.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.380
And, and sometimes the videos that I
thought were really good don't do well

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:46.320
and conversely, that ones that I didn't
think were very good, they do well.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.160
So yeah.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.460
Sometimes you just put up the most
random video, like, "oh, I'll just

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:51.930
put it up just, just for fun."

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.180
And then it gets a million
views or something.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:55.830
What happened?

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:57.910
Million!

00:08:57.910 --> 00:08:58.290
I'm..

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.740
The last million views I think
it was the one I did with you.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:01.910
In, uh, at that park.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:04.610
I'm glad.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:05.120
I'm glad.

00:09:05.189 --> 00:09:05.840
Yeah.

00:09:06.079 --> 00:09:08.449
Yeah, no, it's, it's, it's true.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:09.740
It is amazing.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:14.569
This explosion of the provision of
lessons and content in, in all languages

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.130
that that's available on YouTube.

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:16.910
It's amazing.

00:09:17.329 --> 00:09:22.010
And, and your learners are, are they
aware of like, do they spend a lot of

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:26.420
time on YouTube looking for people like,
not just yourself, but other people

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:29.270
as well uh, as part of their strategy?

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:31.060
Exactly.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:35.339
And that's also one thing that I advocate
is, you know, YouTube has got such a

00:09:35.339 --> 00:09:39.930
plethora of great teachers that if you
only subscribe to one or you only watch

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.650
one person's videos you're missing
out because, you know, I, I'm just

00:09:43.709 --> 00:09:45.830
kind of a one man team, two man team.

00:09:46.275 --> 00:09:51.255
Where I can only put out one video a
week or two videos, but, um, if you've

00:09:51.255 --> 00:09:54.465
subscribed to all the other teachers
in the niche, we're all friends,

00:09:54.465 --> 00:09:58.185
we all like to work together and
collaborate from time to time that you

00:09:58.185 --> 00:10:00.585
can have like 20 videos a week or 30.

00:10:00.795 --> 00:10:01.665
Exactly.

00:10:02.505 --> 00:10:08.805
And so someone who, who subscribes to
you, what happens then YouTube suggests

00:10:09.105 --> 00:10:11.265
sort of related videos on the side?

00:10:11.265 --> 00:10:13.225
Or how do they get from
you to these other...

00:10:14.715 --> 00:10:15.465
I think so.

00:10:15.585 --> 00:10:15.835
Yeah.

00:10:16.755 --> 00:10:21.795
It's a big algorithm, which is still a
mystery to me, but I do believe that if

00:10:21.795 --> 00:10:28.065
you do watch videos in that niche, like
the English learning niche, you do get

00:10:28.065 --> 00:10:33.405
suggested more and more videos about
those topics that you're interested in.

00:10:33.795 --> 00:10:37.025
Do you provide the subtitles
in Japanese or in English?

00:10:37.995 --> 00:10:38.735
That's right.

00:10:38.735 --> 00:10:42.074
One thing that I've been known
for, for my YouTube journey is

00:10:42.074 --> 00:10:43.935
always put in the double subtitles.

00:10:43.935 --> 00:10:47.035
So I put like English on the top
and then Japanese on the bottom.

00:10:47.855 --> 00:10:50.070
And the feedback on
that's been pretty good.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:51.660
Well I'm sure they appreciate it.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:52.980
It's a lot of work to do that.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:53.970
Good on you for doing it.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.310
It takes a long time.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:57.450
Yeah, for sure.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:58.290
Okay.

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:03.120
Well, I'm going to leave a link to
your channel in the description box.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:08.460
And a lot of my viewers are learning
English at different levels and,

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:14.010
uh, I definitely recommend that they
visit your channel and get connected

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:17.520
with Rupa and with other people
who are doing similar things on the

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.440
internet, and Rupa thank you very much.

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:22.410
And, uh, now that the COVID
hopefully is behind us, although

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:26.100
not entirely, hopefully I'll get to
Japan again, or we can have another

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:27.720
meeting in that park in Osaka.

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:31.510
Yeah, we'll go to the same
park and the same Starbucks.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:32.790
In fact, you know

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:32.940
what?

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:34.920
I will leave a link to that video.

00:11:35.895 --> 00:11:36.405
Uh, here.

00:11:36.405 --> 00:11:38.685
So people can go to that
video from this video.

00:11:38.685 --> 00:11:39.765
Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:39.885 --> 00:11:40.425
Okay.

00:11:40.605 --> 00:11:41.715
Thank you very much.

00:11:42.895 --> 00:11:43.705
Thanks so much Steve.

00:11:43.705 --> 00:11:43.985
Okay.

00:11:44.015 --> 00:11:44.505
Thank you.

00:11:44.505 --> 00:11:45.105
Bye-bye.

00:11:46.065 --> 00:11:46.505
Take care.

